WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.620
- [Narrator] As the world grows
in population and prosperity

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.945
we're going to need a
lot more of this stuff.

00:00:05.945 --> 00:00:07.725
(doorbell) (static)

00:00:07.725 --> 00:00:11.080
At the same time, we need to
dramatically reduce emissions.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.530
If only we could stop
emitting so much carbon,

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.113
and put it to use somewhere.

00:00:16.073 --> 00:00:18.488
- Like in this brick?

00:00:18.488 --> 00:00:19.321
(static)

00:00:19.321 --> 00:00:20.580
- If we're gonna
dramatically lower emissions,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.284
we've gotta have a concrete plan.

00:00:22.284 --> 00:00:23.117
(tape failing)

00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:25.180
Get it, concrete plan?

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.150
Ah, nevermind.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.010
(energetic music)

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.820
- [Male] This Great Big Story is by BP.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.743
Fewer emissions, more energy.

00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:35.333
This is the global energy challenge.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.510
- [Narrator] Concrete,
it's built everything

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.900
from Hadrian's Wall,
to the very foundations

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.660
that support this lady,
and her whole city.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.760
But its production and use also emits

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.680
up to 8% of the world's total emissions.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.012
8%, that's a big deal.

00:00:52.012 --> 00:00:52.845
(static)

00:00:52.845 --> 00:00:54.010
The human love affair with concrete

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.440
can be traced back as far as 6500 B.C.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.670
But it was the Romans
who really ran with it,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.100
creating their world building
cement out of volcano ash,

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.840
and made stuff like this,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.300
this, and this.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.650
And here's that Hadrian again.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.330
This time, with the Pantheon.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.230
Show off.
(ping)

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.476
But as Rome fell, the recipe
for this empire building,

00:01:12.476 --> 00:01:16.030
continent conquering group
was lost to the ages,

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:19.200
only to be reinvented 180
years ago by this guy,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.336
and his handlebar mustache.

00:01:20.336 --> 00:01:21.169
(static)

00:01:21.169 --> 00:01:23.430
- You know, it's a market
that's a few thousand years old

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.500
that really has been making
concrete the same way

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.960
for a couple of hundred years.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.680
- EP Henry is a privately held,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.730
family company, fourth generation.

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:33.860
The company was known for innovation

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.700
regarding architectural block design

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.050
for schools, and hospitals,
and universities,

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.123
which we're still doing today.

00:01:40.123 --> 00:01:42.123
(blast)

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.540
- Every time that

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.980
traditional Portland
cement is manufactured you

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:48.956
make a ton of CO2.

00:01:48.956 --> 00:01:49.789
(static)

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:51.090
Concrete is already the second most

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.377
used material on the planet, after water.

00:01:53.377 --> 00:01:56.030
With urbanization and growing population

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.620
more of it's going to
get used, not less of it.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.610
- [Narrator] So what exactly
is the concrete making process?

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.610
- [Tom] If you look at
bread and concrete they

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:04.870
have a lot in common.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:06.930
Basically anywhere in the
world uses essentially

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.100
the same raw materials.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.940
- [Male] You have flour,
eggs and sugar and,

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:10.773
you have milk.

00:02:10.773 --> 00:02:11.606
- [Tom] When you make concrete,

00:02:11.606 --> 00:02:13.310
you've got rocks, you've
got sand, you've got water.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.343
- But with Solidia you use the CO2.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:17.880
- Can I talk about my brick now?

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.713
- [Narrator] Go ahead Vahit.

00:02:18.713 --> 00:02:20.530
- I invented the cement that

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:24.570
can reduce the carbon
footprint of concrete by 70%.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.340
The idea came to me...

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:27.350
here.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:28.980
- [Narrator] Where all the
greatest innovations begin,

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:29.813
right?

00:02:29.813 --> 00:02:30.646
- [Both] Cheers!

00:02:30.646 --> 00:02:32.320
- [Male] Vahit's innovation
was figuring out how to

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.213
mimic mother nature and
make it in a synthetic way

00:02:35.213 --> 00:02:37.580
that allowed us to have
much greater impact.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.090
- [Vahit] I can take that CO2,

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.690
put it in my concrete,

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:42.260
and it's no longer CO2.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:43.690
It's all gone.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:44.523
- [Narrator] So Solidia Concrete

00:02:44.523 --> 00:02:46.720
is made using CO2 instead of water?

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.250
Let's take a closer look.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:50.472
- [Vahit] CO2 Molecule is quite stable but

00:02:50.472 --> 00:02:52.280
when it goes into water,

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.965
the molecular structure
starts getting distorted.

00:02:54.965 --> 00:02:55.798
(static)

00:02:55.798 --> 00:02:57.250
- Then it makes it reactive,

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.828
and then it sees a
calcium ion in the water,

00:02:59.828 --> 00:03:01.160
in the solution.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.960
- Your PH is high enough,

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.570
so it will produce calcium carbonate and

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.000
you will keep consuming the CO2.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.404
- [Narrator] Okay, give us
the simple version, Tom.

00:03:09.404 --> 00:03:10.237
(static)

00:03:10.237 --> 00:03:12.770
- We effectively take it, rip it apart,

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.380
put it back together as something that's

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:15.653
not ever coming out again.

00:03:15.653 --> 00:03:16.486
(static)

00:03:16.486 --> 00:03:18.300
- Today we're able to
run Solidia on this line,

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.220
right here.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.460
- When we produce 1000
square feet of pavers

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:23.950
with Solidia Cement,

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:27.970
that's the equivalent of
someone planting 420 trees,

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:30.280
that's how impactful it is.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.914
It is a better cement for a better planet.

00:03:32.914 --> 00:03:33.747
(static)

00:03:33.747 --> 00:03:35.560
- [Narrator] The CO2 used
in Solidia bricks comes from

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.420
places like whiskey distilleries,

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:38.253
and breweries,

00:03:38.253 --> 00:03:40.330
which would otherwise be
released into the air.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:44.080
Just imagine if the CO2
came from other industries.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.300
- BP has been an important part of

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.240
our board from the beginning.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:52.240
Not only in helping us get the
start up moving as a company,

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.610
but also helping us think
about the carbon mark,

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:55.620
and helping us think about

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.030
the different sources of CO2.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:01.490
- Concrete is the second
largest consumed material on

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:03.010
earth after water.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:06.530
We are talking about 4 billion
tons of cement worldwide.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:07.560
Annual production.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.550
So what we are doing here in Solidia,

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:11.876
I think it will be an
important component of

00:04:11.876 --> 00:04:13.480
the climate challenge.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.000
- [Narrator] This carbon eating concrete

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.030
really could make a big impact.

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:18.260
Well done team!

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.668
Here's to helping build a better future.

00:04:20.668 --> 00:04:23.251
(upbeat music)

